- In C++ you can write comments — notes in your code that the computer ignores. They're there for humans reading the code.
  Commands can be singled-lined or multi-lined 

1.  Single-line comments  (//)
    - To start a comment, type // (two forward slashes).
    - Everything after // up to the end of that line is ignored by the program.
    - The computer skips over it completely — it’s like writing a sticky note on a line of code.

Example: 
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
  // This is a comment
  cout << "Hello World";
  return 0;
}

// Output will be:
   Hello World

2.  Multi-line comments (*/...../*)
    - To start a multi-line comment, type /* (slash + asterisk).
    - To end it, type */ (asterisk + slash).
    - Everything between those two markers is ignored by the program, no matter how many lines it covers.

Example:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
  /* this is comment*/  
  cout << "Hello World";
  return 0;
}

// Output will be:
   Hello World

- Explanation:
  ^ This excerpt is giving you a style recommendation rather than a rule. 
    It's saying: both comment styles work fine, so pick whichever fits the situation — but there's a common convention most programmers follow.
  ^ Use // when your note is short — a word, a phrase, or one line of explanation.
  ^ Use /* ... */ when your note needs more room — several sentences or a whole paragraph.

